WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:12.890
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Hello, everyone.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.890
Welcome to the
session on monitor

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.170
and troubleshoot your
network infrastructure

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.820
with data driven insights.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.070
This is Lakshmi Sharma,
Director of Product Management

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.910
for Networking with my colleague
Irene Abezgauz, Product

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.680
Manager, working on
one of these products

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.570
that you will hear about today.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.490
In this 20-minute
talk, we are going

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:32.590
to discuss the
common user journeys

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.320
during migration,
troubleshooting,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.960
and optimization
of your network.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.050
We will also demonstrate how
Network Intelligence Center,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.650
the product that provides
you tools and capabilities

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:49.540
to support these user journeys,
is helping you by data insights

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.550
and offering a
platform that would

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.460
be used in most effective
ways throughout your user

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.160
journeys of infrastructure,
specifically

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:00.940
networking, troubleshooting,
migration, and optimization.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.790
Network Intelligence
Center is a way

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:09.490
of offering you AI/ML powered
NetOps acceleration platform.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.110
This is Cloud AI Ops in real,
working for your workloads

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.370
onto GCP.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.330
Our goal is to become the
simplest Cloud to operate on

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.540
through an Intelligent
Self-Driving Network that

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:29.230
extends beyond GCP to hybrid,
to multi-cloud, to branches,

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:34.600
to places and capabilities that
you have built for operating

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.050
into virtual and Cloud
journeys, and also integrating

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.260
all the way to legacy
infrastructure and services

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.020
that are built on-prem
or any location

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.570
here henceforth I
mentioned, leveraging all

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:50.170
the AI and the data insights
that we continue to build

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.280
for building GCP and making
GCP the platform driven

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.460
by intelligent insight
and self-driving Cloud.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.460
So Network Intelligence
Center is Google Cloud's way

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.880
of delivering onto that vision
of Self-Driving Network.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.780
It contains multiple
modules to ensure

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.010
you can deploy what
you have intended to.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.740
Basically, it gives you
tools for the deployment

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.090
to be matched to your intent.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.900
It offers you troubleshooting
in any kind of performance

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.750
and connectivity issues
as you're migrating

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.410
or as you're operating
on the GCP, whichever

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.020
location, whichever place,
and whichever application

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.240
you have been running
currently or you

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.340
have been building towards.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.210
It helps you optimize your
network, including firewalls

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.060
and other network services
and infrastructure services

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.570
that you will be using to
deploy your workloads onto GCP.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.420
Networking is the foundation
for all Cloud deployments

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.190
and business processes.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.410
We expect it to be up
and performing well.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.380
Our customers expect it to be.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.110
They want networking
to be seamless,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.090
regardless of the use
case and the journey

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.220
that you are working towards.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.620
User journeys
throughout Cloud, based

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.100
on our experiences of
customers migrating and working

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.180
on GCP with us, specifically
networking scenarios,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.220
there are three
main categories--

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:23.060
planning and migration, day 2
ops at scale, and optimization.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.863
So these are the three sets of
categories and user journeys

00:03:25.863 --> 00:03:27.280
that we will be
taking it through,

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.250
and how different modules and
Network Intelligence Center

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:33.810
help you build and
drive towards that.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:38.880
Here's what you need in
each of those stages.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.040
Early stages,
deployment is live.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.220
So when you're migrating your
network or your workloads,

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.650
while your deployment is live
what you want to make sure

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.067
is that what you intended to
do, what kind of architecture

00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:52.320
you deploy for, is what you're
getting when you have migrated

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.820
or when you're migrating.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.090
And then you also, once you
have measured configuration

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.737
and your intended
[INAUDIBLE],, then you really

00:03:59.737 --> 00:04:01.320
want to make sure
that your traffic is

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.430
flowing as you intended to.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.260
So that's kind of number one
in the early stages of our user

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.090
journey during
planning and migration.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.820
The next step would be
that yes, I have migrated.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.510
Yes, I have it as intended--
configuration and traffic

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.850
as intended, as I
expected it to be.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.480
Now the second phase is
that, is it production ready?

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.690
Is it production live?

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.780
So that's the time when
you want to make sure

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.940
that your network
continues to stay healthy,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.730
you are able to identify
and troubleshoot

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.260
issues in advance--

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.640
not at the time the issue
happens, but prior to

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.160
when the issue has happened.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.110
Or in cases issue
has happened, you

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.960
being able to troubleshoot
it and identify the root

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.690
cause for each of those
issues in operation

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:50.550
or during production
operations very fast

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:55.080
and be able to remedy
it at an instant.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.650
We also want to be able to
optimize your deployment.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.560
You want to be able to optimize
your deployments that you're

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.790
running by having the control
on performance and cost.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:09.120
First you want to be making
sure that your applications are

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.280
running with the
performance and reliability,

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.860
no matter what kind of
optimization that you make--

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.630
configuration
optimization, [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.540
optimization, and services
optimization, maybe

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.740
architecting it again.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.470
So we give you a tool so
that you can optimize it back

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.660
and then you can go back
and check your intent again,

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.660
your traffic patterns
again, and you're back

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.430
to a very healthy
operation cycle.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.460
So for all of
these user journeys

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.790
that we talked through, you need
a lot of data to be available.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.940
You want your data to be
continuously monitored for you.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.120
You want the control on where
my data is, where my logs are,

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.140
where my alerts are--

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.890
you want all of that information
across all your deployments

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:48.900
across these
stages, and you want

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.210
the data monitoring and
telemetry, everything

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:55.230
to be available to you so that
you start to make decisions

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:58.410
in a self-driving and
self-sustainable manner

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.960
without really any
interactions-- to be honest,

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.680
like, literally zero
interaction with people

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.955
on the other side of the Cloud.

00:06:07.955 --> 00:06:11.060
So for all of these data, how
do we bring this data to you?

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.910
Network Intelligence Center
provides that visibility

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:17.270
into working on this network,
how the deployment actually

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:21.290
works, how and where the
traffic flows, and are there

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:25.580
or if there are any problems
and issues with configurations,

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.890
or the traffic, or the
routing-- any kinds

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.130
of [INAUDIBLE] or any anomalies,
or any suboptimalities that you

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:32.400
see.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.560
So once we have all this
information for you,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.160
the next step is for us
to provide users the tools

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.440
to make that data
easily available

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.320
and have the system
intelligently and then

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:46.250
automatically address
any existing issues,

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:50.360
as well as service the user the
most important things, the most

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.340
relevant insights.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.710
We do want to avoid
the noise fatigue.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:58.220
And after all, the network
is a business enabler.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:01.340
We want your focus to be on
the most important and relevant

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:02.580
information.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.090
It allows everything
else to communicate.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.420
Network allows you to
communicate with your users.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.070
Network allows
you to communicate

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:13.010
with your business processes
and your business people.

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:16.490
You want the network to
auto-resolve problems where

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:20.510
they're relevant, and to
service users any issues that

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.820
requires intervention.

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.880
That is why we are working
on enhancing [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:29.480
capability across different
services and capabilities

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:31.490
that we have in
networking to deliver

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:34.460
the most important
information to our users

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.560
across all these
journeys that you put

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.060
in a timely and concise manner.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.180
So we want to be
going from a place

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.410
where you're
planning, migrating,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.220
writing your architecture,
you're making sure

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:48.050
that your intent is
right, then you're

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:51.230
automating and optimizing
toward for your performance

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:52.610
and reliability and cost.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:54.470
Once you have
delivered this cycle,

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:57.320
we want the [INAUDIBLE]
to be working with you so

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.910
that you are able to deliver.

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:00.590
And you want these
capabilities to be

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:05.600
delivered in a self-driving,
self-contained manner to you.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.327
So Day 2 Ops.

00:08:07.327 --> 00:08:09.410
Day 2 Ops capability, which
you see in the middle,

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.180
are what we are focusing in now.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:13.220
And you will see some
capabilities and planning

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:15.540
and migration state
in Day 2 Ops case

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.130
so that you are able to leverage
our products and capabilities

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:22.040
today while we bring
you onto the journey

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:27.092
of eventual optimization and
self-driving capabilities.

00:08:27.092 --> 00:08:28.550
IRENE ABEZGAUZ:
Thank you, Lakshmi.

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.030
So basically, when we
listen to the users,

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:34.620
they emphasize the
following thing.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.010
We want the network
to be up and running.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:39.679
It needs to be available, and
it needs to be performing,

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:41.650
and we have a business to run.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.919
We don't need to deal
with anything else.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:46.070
And essentially, it needs
to be up and running.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.780
If it breaks, we want to
know as soon as possible.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.770
Now, this needs to happen
regardless of what's

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:53.240
happening with our traffic.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.880
It can be spiky.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:00.260
In a nice way to say it, it
could be dynamically changing.

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:03.260
And in those conditions,
we need to be

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:05.300
able to monitor the network.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.930
And essentially,
that's what the users

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.680
have been saying that
Network Intelligence

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.700
Center has been giving them.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.810
And I think the best example,
or the best quote that I

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:18.200
got from this, from one
of our users, was, listen,

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:22.590
we're used to aggressively
monitoring everything on-prem.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:25.220
Now, you take our most
precious workloads

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:28.670
and you put it on this
magical mystery black box,

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:30.920
and we don't want
to lose visibility.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:32.810
We cannot afford
to lose visibility.

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:36.920
And that really
resonated with me, maybe

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:39.200
with the tiny exception
of not wanting

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.930
to bring SNMP to the Cloud
unless I really have to.

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.870
But with the exception
of that, I really

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:48.320
believe in that-- as much
data as possible, and as much

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:50.580
visibility as possible.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:54.980
Now, let's jump right
in and take a look

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.530
at the modules of the
Network Intelligence Center

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.230
and how they're used
in the user journeys

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:03.650
that Lakshmi has described.

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:07.130
The first place where
users typically start

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:09.320
is Network Topology.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:13.580
Basically, they come here when
you've just migrated something,

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.800
or you've created
something from scratch.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:17.650
Now, there is a
new thing, and you

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:21.260
want to make sure that whatever
it is that you've built,

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.330
that it matches your intent.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:25.610
Are those the servers
that I wanted to have?

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:28.610
Are they communicating in
the way that I expected?

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.530
How is traffic following?

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:32.740
How is it flowing?

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:36.920
Are the relevant
regions being served

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.900
from relevant continents?

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.060
Do I have any inter-regional
communications

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:42.620
that I'm not supposed to have?

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:46.280
Am I bypassing load balancers
when I shouldn't be?

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:47.990
All those things
are basically things

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:50.090
that you see in
Network Topology,

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:53.240
with the addition of being
able to see performance

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:55.440
metrics on the edges.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.660
Now, from here, if you're
trying to get more information,

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.740
there is actually a direct
pivot from Network Topology

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.420
into VPC Flow Logs.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:08.210
So if you are looking
at a specific server

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:12.410
at a specific edge, and you
want to get more Swiss knife

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:14.960
kind of information,
you can just

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.390
go directly from
here into VPC Flow

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:20.960
Logs in the relevant context
and see what's happening.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.460
Now, in Network
Topology, you can

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:26.090
see compute instances,
load balancers, connections

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:28.760
to on-prem, Cloud APIs.

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:33.140
We are going to add GKE
infrastructure support.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:37.700
And that's essentially Topology,
typically used in migration

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:41.000
or where something changes.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.030
Now, the next thing is
the Performance Dashboard.

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:47.660
This is where you end
up when things are not

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:49.340
going quite as expected.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.110
Usually, when you see
there is a problem

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.840
or you suspect the
problem, you'll go here.

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:58.100
And here, you can actually see
packet loss and latency metrics

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.410
for traffic in your projects.

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:05.630
There is a combination here of
synthetic traffic and sampling

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:08.900
of actual user traffic
of your machines.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:12.950
You will see here only zones,
regions where you are deployed.

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:16.880
And you can essentially see
your own specific performance.

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:22.870
Now, one thing that we
have recently launched

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:25.390
is the Global
Performance Dashboard.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:28.480
In many cases, when
there is a problem

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.280
and you get to the
Performance Dashboard,

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:32.620
the question you're
going to ask yourself

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.990
is, OK, something is
not going as expected.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:39.400
I'm seeing high latency, or
I'm seeing high packet loss.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:40.570
OK?

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:45.070
For that matter, is the problem
anywhere in my deployment,

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:48.400
or is it a Google
Cloud-wide kind of problem?

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:52.420
And having those two Performance
Dashboards side-by-side

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:55.870
basically lets you compare
your project performance

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:57.640
to the performance
of all of Google.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.220
And then you can know whether
there is a network outage,

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:04.480
or maybe there is just a few
servers choking somewhere--

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:07.990
100% CPU-- and that is the
reason why they're not properly

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:09.145
responding to requests.

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:14.520
Any Google-wide
outages are going

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:17.670
to be very clearly visible
on both dashboards.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:21.270
And obviously, when you pivot
from your project to this one,

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:25.215
you'll be able to see where
is the source of that problem.

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:34.640
Now, as I said, we are going
from relatively high-level--

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:36.090
OK, I just upgraded.

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.520
Please tell me in high-level
how everything looks,

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:41.380
because I'm trying to understand
the connection between things.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:44.160
And from there, you
can actually drill down

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.230
to very specific metrics.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:50.970
As I said, it really actually
depends on the type of user.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:51.470
Right?

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:53.000
Some users, they're
going to say,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.180
listen, just throw all
the information you can.

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.670
I'm going to derive all
the insights myself.

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:00.230
Just give me all
the data possible.

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.930
I'm just going to plug it
into a third-party system,

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:04.140
or I'm going to process it.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:06.940
And I want to see raw data.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:08.890
And other types
of users are just

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:10.840
going to say, please
crunch everything for me,

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:14.350
and please only call me if
it's super critical, super

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:16.720
important, and you're
absolutely sure that this

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:17.980
requires my attention.

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.800
Now, for the kind
of users who want

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:25.870
to see everything and want
to get this visibility,

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:27.670
there in-Cloud monitoring.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:29.800
There are very specific
product metrics.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:34.510
You can see, for example, for
every VPN in every project.

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:37.480
You can see the state
of the VPN tunnel.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:39.680
Are there any issues?

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:42.580
You can see whether the
tunnel is up or not,

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:44.650
have there been any
Handshake problems,

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:45.760
what's the throughput?

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:47.440
For Interconnect as
well, you can see

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:48.790
the status of the attachment.

00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:49.990
You can see the throughput.

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:52.750
You can see the throughput
over a period of time.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.760
Essentially, you can go from
very kind of high-level,

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:59.320
this is your Topology, to
very, very specific details.

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:03.880
And I think this is the goal
of really providing something

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:07.040
that we can proudly
call data-driven, right?

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.740
OK, here's all the data.

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:11.890
And you can crunch it yourself,
or we can crunch it for you

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:15.250
and get to a point.

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:17.040
Now, another-- this
is actually one

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:20.340
of the favorite
products by our users,

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.640
saving a huge amount
of support calls.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:25.830
And it goes like this.

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:28.470
Give me endpoint A.
Give me endpoint B.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:33.450
And this product checks whether
the configuration allows

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:36.270
connectivity between
these two endpoints.

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:40.200
This means essentially, if
you're looking at VM to VM,

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:45.240
nodes to master, VM to Cloud
SQL, on-prem to Cloud SQL,

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:48.870
is your configuration actually
allowing this connectivity?

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:52.560
And then if it's not
allowing this connectivity,

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:54.750
basically, this module
is going to tell you

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.760
there is a firewall
rule blocking it,

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.040
or you have a routing issue.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.690
Now, these look
at configuration.

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.950
And what we have just
recently launched

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:08.700
is dynamic connectivity
tests, which

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:10.530
for quite a few
of the scenarios,

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:12.270
they also look at
what's actually

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:13.490
happening on the data plane.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:16.320
OK, so your configuration
allows connectivity.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:21.510
Now let's take a deeper dive
and look at the metrics.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.520
What is the latency
between these two?

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.960
What is the packet loss ratio,
if any, between these two?

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:30.930
You have the connectivity
test to basically tell you

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:35.070
whether any two
endpoints are able to

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.140
or should be able to
for configuration,

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:40.170
and are actually able to
connect and talk to each other,

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:42.690
and how that
connection looks like.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:46.230
Now, Network Intelligence
Center has additional modules,

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.300
like Firewall
Insights, for example.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:52.830
That's going to tell you whether
you have unused firewall rules,

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:54.730
or any additional information.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:56.370
So there is quite a
lot there that you

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:59.280
can go and actually check out.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:05.589
And a quick recap.

00:17:05.589 --> 00:17:07.740
In this not-very-long
presentation,

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:10.950
we've talked about what we have
seen to be the typical user

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:13.950
journeys when users
are migrating,

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:17.640
when they're actually
operating at very large scales,

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:21.770
and when they're trying
to do optimization.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:26.690
And now, since this
is a recorded session,

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:28.910
I want to share with you
some of the questions

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:34.010
that we're usually being
asked, and the answer to these.

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.590
Probably the most
common question

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:38.690
I get is, what about GKE?

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:42.950
And the answer is that yes,
essentially, the Performance

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:46.430
Dashboards show the
performance for GKE cheeky

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:48.290
workloads as well.

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:51.470
We have GKE connectivity
in Connectivity Test.

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:53.840
As I mentioned, in
Topology, we are

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:56.840
working on showing the
GKE infrastructure.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:58.820
That is a big part
of the workloads that

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:01.550
are running on Google
Cloud, and that's definitely

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:03.210
something to be covered.

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:07.110
We're also looking at
serverless-- for example,

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:07.970
not just GKE.

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:09.200
Again, the same support.

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.160
You have Topology.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:11.390
You have Connectivity Test.

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:14.160
So there's quite a lot
that you can learn.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:18.850
This is definitely
not just for Compute.

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:22.750
Another super common question,
especially by the larger

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:28.510
organizations, is how does this
fit into my existing processes,

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:33.640
because I am hybrid, because
I'm the Cloud, because I already

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:35.620
set up a thing, and
I want to continue

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:38.730
using that thing, which
makes a lot of sense?

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:42.050
So basically, we're looking
at it from two aspects.

00:18:42.050 --> 00:18:45.820
One aspect is taking all the
information in the Network

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:47.830
Intelligence Center
and making it

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:50.480
available to be consumed
programmatically.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:51.820
So you can grab it via API.

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:53.630
You can plug it into
your own systems.

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:56.500
So you can trigger any sort
of response based on alerts,

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:59.740
for example, which you've
set, and so on and so on.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.770
The other one is that the
combination of Network

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:05.020
Intelligence Center
and Cloud Monitoring

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:09.400
basically allows you to
see all your monitoring

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:11.590
in a single place.

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:15.130
Last question, as I see
we are approaching time,

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:18.830
is can this be used
for compliance?

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:22.180
And that's actually one of
the major goals of the Network

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:23.290
Intelligence Center.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:25.240
You can see where
traffic is flowing,

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:27.400
or for [INAUDIBLE]
purposes, you can see

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:29.770
where traffic is not flowing.

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:32.860
You can get VPC Flow
Logs on the trail.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:34.960
You can get screenshots
that you can then

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:36.320
use in compliance reports.

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:40.360
So there is actually quite a lot
in mind in terms of compliance,

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:42.370
and quite a lot that
the Network Intelligence

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:45.040
Center can deliver.

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:48.610
This team is always available
for any additional questions

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:50.020
that you may have.

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:52.660
There is also quite
a lot happening

00:19:52.660 --> 00:19:55.190
in the Next '21
networking sessions,

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:58.180
and I do encourage you to
go ahead and take a look.

00:19:58.180 --> 00:19:59.940
Thank you.

